<h2>Frequently Asked Questions:</h2>
<h3><strong>Do I need an electrician for low voltage lighting?</strong></h3>
<p>Low voltage lighting systems are generally considered safe enough for DIY installation, making them a popular choice for homeowners. However, if your layout is complex or involves multiple zones and transformers, it’s worth bringing in a professional. Hiring a licensed electrician ensures everything is installed safely, efficiently, and to a higher standard of finish.</p>
<h3><strong>How deep should landscape lighting wire be buried?</strong></h3>
<p>In most cases, landscape lighting wire should be buried between 2 to 6 inches deep. This is enough to protect the wiring from everyday damage such as foot traffic, lawn mowing, and general wear while keeping it neatly out of sight. That said, the ideal depth can vary depending on your local climate, soil conditions, and the specific layout of your outdoor space.</p>
<h3><strong>How many lights can I run on one transformer?</strong></h3>
<p>The number of lights you can run on a single transformer depends entirely on the combined wattage of all your fixtures. Your transformer must be rated to handle that total load comfortably without being pushed to its limit. Calculating this correctly before installation is important, as an overloaded transformer can cause voltage drop, dimming, and long-term damage to your system.</p>
<h3><strong>Is low voltage landscape lighting worth it?</strong></h3>
<p>Absolutely. Low voltage landscape lighting enhances the safety, beauty, and overall value of your property in one upgrade. It extends the usability of your outdoor spaces well into the evening hours, creating a welcoming atmosphere for both residents and guests. On top of that, its energy efficiency makes it a smart and cost-effective long-term investment compared to standard outdoor lighting options.</p>

